"""
CSTB Epilepsy — Unverricht-Lundborg Disease (EPM1 / Cystatin B / Dodecamer-Repeat-Expansion / Progressive Myoclonic Epilepsy)
=============================================================================================================================
40-patient cohort · CSTB (21q22.3) · Autosomal recessive LOF
CSTB = Cystatin B (also CST6, Stefin B); encodes intracellular cysteine protease inhibitor
LOF → unregulated cathepsin B/H/L/S activity → lysosomal membrane permeability → oxidative stress
→ mitochondrial dysfunction → progressive cerebellar Purkinje cell and cortical neuron death
→ progressive myoclonic epilepsy, cerebellar ataxia — Unverricht-Lundborg Disease (ULD)

CSTB PROTEIN BIOLOGY (CYSTATIN B):
CSTB (21q22.3), Cystatin B (Stefin B):
  - 98 amino acids, ~11 kDa (smallest cystatin family member)
  - Family: Type I cystatin (Stefin subfamily, no signal peptide, no disulfide bonds)
  - Structure: α-helix + β-sheet; N-terminal wedge-loop + β-hairpin form tripartite
      cathepsin-binding interface; Forms tight-binding reversible complexes with:
      Cathepsin B (Ki ~0.35 nM), Cathepsin H (Ki ~1.5 nM), Cathepsin L (Ki ~0.23 nM),
      Cathepsin S (Ki ~3.0 nM)
  - Localisation: primarily cytoplasmic; also nuclear; released into lysosomes under
      oxidative stress → inhibits lysosomal cathepsins escaping into cytoplasm
  - Function: "cytoplasmic safety net" against lysosomal cathepsin leakage;
      prevents cathepsin-mediated cytoplasmic and nuclear protein degradation;
      protects mitochondria from cathepsin B/L-mediated outer membrane permeabilisation;
      anti-apoptotic (cathepsin B/L → cytochrome c release → caspase cascade)
  - Homohexamer: cytoplasmic cystatin B forms hexamers at physiological concentrations;
      hexamer has lower cathepsin affinity but higher stability; hexamer ↔ monomer
      equilibrium regulates available inhibitor pool
  - CSTB LOF: unregulated cathepsin activity → oxidative stress → mitochondrial
      dysfunction → neuronal death; Purkinje cells especially vulnerable (high cathepsin
      activity + limited regenerative capacity); cortical neurons (myoclonus generator)

UNVERRICHT-LUNDBORG DISEASE (ULD) — PROGRESSIVE MYOCLONIC EPILEPSY TYPE 1:
  Onset: 6-13 years (mean ~10 years); PREVIOUSLY NORMAL development (cardinal feature)
  Cardinal features:
    (1) Progressive myoclonic epilepsy — ACTION MYOCLONUS most disabling feature;
        myoclonus worsened by intentional movement (writing, eating), light touch, auditory
        startle; stimulus-sensitive (reflex myoclonus); cortical origin (giant SEP, >8 mV)
    (2) GTCS — generalised tonic-clonic seizures, often nocturnal; less frequent than myoclonus
    (3) Cerebellar ataxia — progressive; dysmetria, intention tremor, gait ataxia;
        SLOWER PROGRESSION than myoclonus; distinguishes from other spinocerebellar ataxias
    (4) MILD cognitive decline — MUCH MILDER than Lafora; some patients cognitively
        intact to 4th-5th decade; cognitive decline worsened by drug burden and depression
    (5) Photosensitivity — 70% photoparoxysmal response; less prominent than Lafora
    (6) NOT UNIFORMLY FATAL — patients can survive to 4th-7th decade; prognosis BETTER
        than Lafora (EPM2A/NHLRC1) but WORSE than genetic generalised epilepsy
  ULD vs Lafora (EPM2A/NHLRC1) key distinctions:
    ULD: earlier onset (6-13y) vs Lafora (10-18y); NO Lafora bodies (no polyglucosan);
    ULD: milder cognitive trajectory; Lafora: dementia within 5-10y;
    ULD: NOT universally fatal; Lafora: universally fatal within 10y;
    ULD: skin biopsy NEGATIVE for polyglucosan; Lafora: pathognomonic positive;
    ULD: CSTB dodecamer expansion on molecular testing (90% of alleles)
  ULD vs MERRF (MTTK, mitochondrial):
    ULD AR nuclear; MERRF maternal mitochondrial; ULD: no ragged-red fibres;
    MERRF: elevated CSF lactate; mtDNA m.8344A>G; hearing loss, cardiac
  Epidemiology: ~1:20,000 in Finland (Baltic Sea endemic); ~1:100,000 global;
    Founder mutations in Finnish (dodecamer repeat + R68X), Mediterranean,
    North African, Japanese populations

CONTRAINDICATIONS UNIQUE TO CSTB / UNVERRICHT-LUNDBORG DISEASE:
  1. CARBAMAZEPINE / OXCARBAZEPINE / PHENYTOIN — ABSOLUTE CI:
     Na-channel blockers WORSEN myoclonic seizures paradoxically in ALL PME types including ULD;
     interneuron disinhibition → myoclonic circuit amplification; NOT disease-specific
     (same CI as Lafora, MERRF, Gaucher-related PME); Emergency note: IV fosphenytoin
     ABSOLUTELY CONTRAINDICATED for SE in ULD — use IV LEV + BDZ;
     WORSE in ULD: action myoclonus already most disabling feature — CBZ/PHT trigger acute
     myoclonic escalation that may persist for days after exposure
  2. LAMOTRIGINE — HIGH RISK (MYOCLONIC WORSENING):
     LTG Na-channel block at inhibitory interneurons → disinhibition → myoclonic worsening;
     documented in ULD (Majoie 2005, Epilepsia) and JME/myoclonic-atonic epilepsy;
     avoid LTG monotherapy in ULD; if GTCS require LTG adjunct, use ONLY with VPA
     (VPA partially blunts LTG myoclonic-worsening effect)
  3. TIAGABINE — ABSOLUTE CI:
     GAT-1 block → perisynaptic GABA accumulation → GABA-A desensitisation → NCSE;
     ULD progressive GABAergic dysfunction (Purkinje cell death → diminished cerebellar
     GABA output to thalamus) amplifies cortical NCSE susceptibility;
     NCSE in ULD may present as WORSENING ACTION MYOCLONUS or apparent seizure escalation
     rather than classical tonic-clonic NCSE → clinically misidentified without EEG
  4. VIGABATRIN — HIGH RISK (AVOID):
     Irreversible visual field defects (30-40% cumulative); VGB-induced retinal GABA-T
     inhibition disproportionately toxic in photosensitive patients (70% ULD);
     paradoxical myoclonic worsening in PME via complex GABAergic rebound effects
  5. GABAPENTIN / PREGABALIN — WORSEN MYOCLONUS:
     Calcium channel α2δ modulation → paradoxical myoclonic worsening in PME;
     documented in ULD (Crespel 1999) and myoclonic epilepsies; avoid in ULD;
     distinguish from ULD overlap with neuropathic pain (cerebellar tremor/ataxia may
     present as pain complaint) — GBP contraindicated for pain if ULD diagnosis confirmed
  6. VPA without POLG1 SCREEN — ABSOLUTE CI:
     Fatal Alpers-Huttenlocher hepatotoxicity in biallelic POLG1 + VPA;
     CSTB (21q22.3) and POLG1 (15q26.1) are unlinked — coincidental POLG1 carriers exist;
     mandatory POLG1 screen before VPA initiation in ALL PME patients including ULD

GENETICS:
  Gene:        CSTB (21q22.3) — Cystatin B / Stefin B / CST6
  Protein:     Cystatin B; 98 aa ~11 kDa; intracellular type-I cystatin
  Inheritance: Autosomal recessive LOF; biallelic mutations required
  pLI:         0.0 (highly recessive; heterozygous LOF well-tolerated)
  OMIM:        *601145 (CSTB gene); #254800 (EPM1 / Unverricht-Lundborg Disease)
  Mutation type: ~90% DODECAMER REPEAT EXPANSION in 5' UTR/promoter (CCCCGCCCCGCG)n;
    Normal: <3 repeats; Carriers: 6-9 repeats (premutation); Affected: >30 repeats
    (typically 40-100+); expansion reduces CSTB transcription ~95%
    ~10% point mutations: R68X (Finnish founder), Q71X, W78*, splice-site variants
  Founder mutations:
    Finnish/Baltic: large dodecamer expansion (40+ repeats) + R68X compound het
    Mediterranean: point mutations (Q71X, splice-site)
    North African: specific frameshift/missense
    Japanese: rare; distinct founder mutations
  Incidence:   ~1:20,000 (Finland); ~1:100,000 worldwide
  First report: Unverricht H 1891 (Monatsschrift Psychiatrie Neurologie) — phenotype;
               Berkovic SF et al. 1991 (Ann Neurol) — linked to chromosome 21;
               Pennacchio LA et al. 1996 (Science) — CSTB gene + dodecamer expansion
  Fatal:       NOT universally fatal; 4th-7th decade survival common;
               Cause of death: aspiration pneumonia, SUDEP, falls/trauma, iatrogenic (CBZ/PHT)
  Diagnosis:   CSTB repeat expansion PCR (primary method) + sequencing for point mutations;
               Skin biopsy: NEGATIVE for polyglucosan (key distinguisher from Lafora);
               Giant SEP (>8 mV amplitude) pathognomonic cortical correlate of action myoclonus

KEY STANDARDS:
  ILAE-2022  PME classification and genetic testing guidelines
  NICE-NG217 Epilepsy management in children and adults (UK)
  Kalviainen-2008 Unverricht-Lundborg disease natural history (Epilepsia)
  Kälviäinen-2012 ULD management consensus (Nat Rev Neurol)
  Canafoglia-2020 ULD long-term outcome series (Epilepsia)
  CPIC-POLG1-2023 VPA contraindication in POLG1 carriers
  MHRA-VPPP-2021 Valproate Pregnancy Prevention Programme
  Pennacchio-1996-Science CSTB discovery
  Berkovic-1991-AnnNeurol ULD chromosome 21 linkage

KEY PHARMACOLOGICAL DISTINCTIONS:
  1. PIRACETAM LEVEL A — MOST EFFECTIVE IN ULD AMONG ALL PME:
     Action myoclonus in ULD is cortical reflex myoclonus;
     piracetam at high doses (24-45 g/day adult) provides the strongest Level A evidence
     anti-myoclonic effect in ULD (stronger relative benefit than in Lafora where disease
     progression overwhelms treatment); mechanism: positive allosteric AMPA modulation
     at high concentrations reduces cortical myoclonic circuit excitability;
     multiple RCT evidence (Brown 1993, Harding 1995 — piracetam in PME);
     start 2.4 g/day, escalate to 24-45 g/day over weeks; IV piracetam for acute myoclonic SE;
     no hepatotoxicity, no drug interactions — safest add-on in complex ULD polypharmacy
  2. VPA BACKBONE + LEV ADJUNCT — DUAL-MECHANISM COMBINATION:
     VPA (sodium valproate): GABAergic + Na-channel + Ca-channel → broad PME coverage;
     LEV (levetiracetam): SV2A modulation → specific anti-myoclonic mechanism;
     VPA + LEV combination is more effective than either alone in ULD (Coppola 2004 series);
     LEV 1000-3000 mg/day in 2 divided doses; VPA 1500-3000 mg/day;
     LEV behavioural toxicity (irritability, aggression) lower in ULD than in DEE/ASD patients
     (ULD patients typically cognitively preserved = good behavioural tolerance)
  3. CBZ/PHT/OXC ABSOLUTE CI — WORSE FUNCTIONAL IMPACT IN ULD THAN OTHER PME:
     In ULD, action myoclonus is the PRIMARY FUNCTIONAL DISABILITY (writing, eating, gait);
     CBZ/PHT administration → acute myoclonic escalation disproportionately impairs function
     even at single dose; patients may lose ability to write/eat for days after CBZ exposure;
     functional regression from iatrogenic Na-blocker more VISIBLE and prolonged in ULD
     than in Lafora (where disease progression dominates); document as medical alert
  4. BROMIDE — HISTORICAL FIRST PME TREATMENT (STILL USED IN REFRACTORY ULD):
     Potassium bromide was the FIRST effective treatment for Unverricht-Lundborg disease
     (Unverricht himself used bromide in 1891!); remains legitimate rescue option in
     VPA-intolerant or POLG1-positive ULD patients; mechanism: Cl- channel modulation
     (Br- substitutes for Cl- in GABA-A channels → enhanced inhibition);
     dose: 1-3 g/day potassium bromide; limitations: bromism (cognitive dulling,
     acne, dermatitis) at higher doses; renal excretion — adjust in renal impairment;
     use as 3rd/4th-line rescue if LEV + VPA + piracetam inadequate
  5. NOT DISEASE-MODIFYING (UNLIKE LAFORA) — SYMPTOMATIC MANAGEMENT ONLY:
     Unlike Lafora (EPM2A: metformin + KD disease-modifying), NO disease-modifying
     therapy exists for CSTB LOF; cathepsin B inhibitors (e.g., E64) in animal models
     but no human trial; gene therapy/ASO approaches in early preclinical phase;
     management = optimised symptomatic seizure control + functional rehabilitation;
     physiotherapy, occupational therapy, speech therapy = CORE management alongside AEDs
  6. PHYSIOTHERAPY + OCCUPATIONAL THERAPY AS PRIMARY DISEASE MANAGEMENT:
     Action myoclonus and cerebellar ataxia in ULD respond to physical rehabilitation;
     weighted gloves/utensils reduce action myoclonus amplitude during tasks;
     ankle-foot orthoses (AFO) for ataxic gait; assistive technology for writing;
     ULD patients are cognitively preserved → motivation for rehabilitation high;
     occupational therapy for adaptations (weighted cutlery, non-slip mats, voice dictation);
     contrast with Lafora: rehabilitation less relevant due to rapid cognitive decline
"""

